Date: Fri, 27 Aug 93 10:50:14 PDT
From: edgar@spectrx.saigon.com (Edgar W. Swank)
Subject: INVEST: Re:what to do with just a little money?

Elizabeth Schwartz asked:

    I'm currently tucking a couple of hundred dollars a month into a
    savings account at roughly 3.5% interest, trying to get a large
    enough lump to play with.  The state is, in lieu of social
    security (which we state workers are exempt from) tucking 8% of my
    before-tax income into a fund which I will get back when I leave.
    The State is also vacuuming a huge amount of taxes since I have no
    deductions, shelters, or excuses.

You are lucky to be exempt from S.S.  The first thing to ask, is if
you can voluntarily increase the before-tax income contributed either
to this fund you mentioned or to another fund under a 401K plan. 401K
is also before-tax money, or more acurately, tax-deferred, since you
will pay taxes when you withdraw the money.  If your employer has a
401K plan, contribute the maximum you can!!  Some employers will even
contribute matching amounts; this is free money!!

If, when you switch to a private employer, be sure to ask about their
401K plan.

If you have any control over how either the S.S.-substitute fund or
the 401K fund is invested, select a well-diversified stock fund, such
as one that claims to match the performance of the S&P 500.  At your
age, it's appropriate for you to take some investment risk, since you
have time to recoup losses, if any. Also, by investing regularly, you
will gain the benefits of "dollar cost averaging"; that is, you will
buy more when your fund price is low and less when it is high; so you
will make money even if all the fund price does is fluctuate both up
and down from your initial price and end up at the same price.  Of
course, you can still lose money if the fund price enters a long-term
down trend.

You also might consider investing in an IRA.  You invest after-tax
dollars, but all the interest & capital gains you receive is
tax-deferred. There's a lot of paperwork to keep track of which
portions are before and after-tax.  There are also custodial fees to
pay.  Check Charles Schwab & other discount brokers to see who has
the lowest (or no) custodial fees.

Another tax shelter (& there aren't many left) is a municipal bond
fund. I have some money in Cal Muni Fund.  Since these are medium &
long-term bonds, the fund value (principal) fluctuates up and down
against interest rates. But these are returning a tax-free income of
about 5% per annum, which is better than the before-tax income of most
money market funds.  Note I said tax-FREE, not tax-deferred, and, (if
you pick a fund designed for your state) your dividends are free from
both state and federal income taxes.  In some unusual situations, you
could be caught by the alternate minimum tax, so check that out before
investing most of your money in this one area.  As with stock funds,
always pick a no-load municipal bond fund (like Cal Muni).

    These two pockets of money are my total worldly assets, including
    potential retirement, which at age 33 I am starting to think
    about.

    What would you do with a small amount of money?  I notice that the
    savings bank pays more interest on their straight savings than
    they do on a money market fund.  I don't have enough yet to get
    into the big funds, and I don't have enough to buy stock unless I
    can buy cheap.  If I bought 20 $50 shares, there's a $25 fee to
    buy and $25 to sell plus a surcharge on profitsm, and taxes on the
    profits, so it's hard to make money on it unless the stock goes up
    by a fair bit.

I would stay out of individual stocks.  You can invest in a good,
no-load (no commission) diversified stock fund.  Several funds, I
think, accept as little as $1000 to open an account and as little as
$100 for additions.  I currently have a little "Schwab 5000", which
you can buy commission free (and sell commission free after holding
for six months) from any Schwab & Co. office.

Once your total net worth exceeds $10,000 or $20,000, you might
consider one of my favorite investments, brokered mortgage loans. A
Real Estate broker accepts a loan application from a home owner whose
credit isn't quite good enough to get a bank loan.  The broker then
finds investors who are willing to loan the money secured by the
borrowers home (or other real property). These may be either first or
second mortgages.  The borrower typically pays all fees and
commissions. The broker usually "services" the loan for a small
fee, typically 1/2 percent of the interest rate.  So a loan quoted to
a borrower of say 12% would pay a net of 11.5% to the investor(s). The
investors may (always ask!) also be entitled to any late charges or
pre-payment penalties.

The borrower pays for an appraisal of his property and can typically
borrow 60-70% of his "equity", the value of the property less any
senior loans.  Since these borrowers' credit is not the best, you can
expect late payments from time to time. Occasionaly, a foreclosure and
sale of the borrower's property may be necessary. Usually the broker
will take care of this for you. Your risk is that the value of the
property after foreclosure will be less than your investment. This can
happen if the real estate market falls more then 25% or so during the
term of the investment, but this is a very unusual situation. Even
then, you could expect to get most of your money back when the
property is sold. In this kind of economic depression, you might
expect stocks to do even worse.

A loan broker can combine up to ten investors into a single loan (here
in CA anyway). Large investors can be combined with smaller investors,
so it's possible to invest less than 10% of a loan.  It's usually
possible to invest as little as $5000.  Sometimes brokers will even
allow an investment as small as $2000 in connection with an IRA
account.

The loan brokers can also handle money invested in an IRA through a
bank trustee. Expect high custodial fees, but the higher
(fully taxable) interest you get from these investments may make the
fees worth it.  I personally stopped making IRA contributions when the
contributions became non-deductible. But I left in what I had already
contributed and that's all invested in these mortgage loans.

I would advise avoiding the mortgage loan category called
"construction loans", even though these have a slightly higher
interest rate. Your security is a piece of bare land upon which the
builder is supposed to build (a) house(s).  If he defaults before the
house(s) are built, you may have a problem selling the bare land (or
worse, land with half-built houses which may have to be torn down).
I got "burned" in a couple of these; I'm still trying to sell one
piece of bare land (along with other investors).

    I don't have the ready cash to buy a house, and the out-of-pocket
    expenses exceed the gain in value for quite a few years.  I figure
    I'd need about $5,000 to buy, and the same available in case of
    major repairs.

In hindsight, a house or any real property investment has not had a
very good return in recent years. You would have been better off
renting and saving the difference between rent and house payments, as
well as your down payment, repairs, etc.  But this will not continue
forever.  Inflation will return. Government cannot resist the
temptation to debase the currency for long. When inflation returns,
real estate can be expected to inflate faster than general prices.

Don't buy a house to live in until you are reasonably assured to
remain in one area for at least five years. Otherwise commissions will
eat up any profits. Some companies (private) will pay your sales costs
for you if they relocate you at their request. Ask about that.

    So, how would you turn a small amount of money into a medium sized
    amount?  I'm thinking that I should try to put most of it
    someplace stable (money market?)  and try some small-scale
    entrepreneurship with the leftovers.  I have another year of night
    school and can't tackle anything ambitious until then (I also can
    go back to working for a for-profit corporation after that, which
    should increase my benefits substantially!)

Avoid "small-scall entrepreneurship".  Most small businesses fail
within a short time.  A possible exception is a franchise. Even then,
expect to start out working long hours for little pay.

    Where do you moderate-income extropians have your money?

About 7% in an interest-bearing (2%?) checking account.
Perhaps 6% in a CD at 4.28%
Less than 2% in the Schwab 5000 fund
About 6% in Cal Muni tax-free fund.
About 4% in a single-premium Life Insurance Policy
         (growth is tax-deferred)
About 4% in my IRA.
About 9% in a 401K.
About 12% in mortgage loans.
About 17% in my residence, which I now own free & clear.
About 20% in rental real estate, which I own with partners.
          I bought this back when it was a better tax shelter,
          and I needed a tax shelter. I expect to sell some or
          most of this in the next five years.
About 13% "Other" (Car, personal property, ...)

The above is my opinion.  No guarantees offered.  If others disagree,
they are welcome to post their arguments.

--
