Date: Fri, 27 Aug 1993 17:24:57 -0400
From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: Intellectual Property, ppl, etc.

David Friedman wrote:
>Anarcho-capitalism, at least of the sort I have described, is built on
>pairwise contracts, and workable in part for that reason. A global contract
>in which everyone in the world agrees to a change in legal rules because it
>produces net benefits seems implausible because of the transaction costs of
>getting unanimous agreement. In my system, the pairwise contracts are
>actually negotiated by protection agencies for lots of customers at once,
>but conceptually it is as if each pair of individuals negotiated the legal
>rule applying between them.

How does this 'as if' work? If there existed protection agencies of every
imaginable variety, then my choice of PA would be equivalent to my
negotiation of a contract. But there won't be every imaginable variety, so
my choices will be more constrained. The actions of the PAs are similarly
constrained by what is feasible, cost-effective, and marketable. Seems more
like a mass contract to me. If pairwise contracting did occur, transactions
costs would probably move things toward some fairly standard contracts,
with people only negotiating over special things. Does the PA simulate
this? Are those the only useful granularities of contract - pairwise and
universal?

>[. . .] Suppose we start in a world where B has no such
>obligation [to respect A's intellectual property], and change to one where he
>>has the obligation. [. . .] The change has four effects:
>[. . .] 1 is a wash--B loses what A gains. 2 and 3 are
>net losses. 4 is a [tiny] net gain.
>
>I believe it follows from this argument that the costs of the legal change
>(2 and 3) are included entire in the calculation, but only a small fraction
>of the benefit (equal to 1/(1+the number of people who have not agreed to
>respect A's property rights). So unless benefits are enormously greater
>than costs or the population is tiny, we would not expect the legal change
>to occur--even if it is efficient.

IMHO, all this shows is that non-protection of intellectual property is
evolutionarily stable. Protection may be similarly stable, and is more
likely to be the initial position, since intellectual property is enforced
now throughout much of the world. (Will PPL start everything from scratch?)

Suppose we start in a world where B has an obligation to respect A's
intellectual property, and change to one where he has none.
 
1. B is richer and A poorer by whatever licensing fee B used to pay A for
the use of any of A's intellectual property B chooses to use.

2. B is richer and nobody is poorer as a result of B choosing to use
some of A's intellectual property that A had priced at more
than its value to B. 

3. A and B are richer by whatever they save on negotiating and enforcing
the licensing agreement.

4. A and B are poorer as a result of the decreased amount of intellectual
property that A produces, due to his being unable to collect license fees
from B.

1 is still a wash. 2 and 3 are net gains. 4 is a [tiny] net loss. But note
that to A, 1 is a loss, 2 a wash, 3 is a tiny gain, and 4 is a loss. Why
would A agree to this change in the arrangement? So intellectual proerty
enforcement is stable from a pairwise contracting viewpoint. 

But is it stable from the viewpoint of competing PA's? Will a PA that does
not enforce intellectual property compete successfully against competitors
that do?

The innovating PA will be able to offer a lower price to its customers, due
to savings it gains from not enforcing intellectual property rights. My
intuition says that the Anarchic Booksellers Assn. would try to make an
offer to this PA, providing it a slightly higher profit for not offering
the alternative service. So the owners of intellectual property would end
up paying for all enforcement, and no difference in price to consumers
could be generated by scrimping on enforcement. Since most of the costs and
benefits of intellectual property get shifted to the publishers, they are
in a good position to make the efficient decision. There is a potential
collective action problem, in that the beneficiaries of intellectual
property may not form an association, or may under- or over-estimate the
benefit of intellectual property enforcement.

What if some ideological PA refused to take the ABA's offer and insisted on
sheilding its customers from enforcement of intellectual property? Would
such a PA prosper and gain immitators? 

The ABA could use a variant of the strategy above, but give the bonus to
the competitors of the renegade PA, allowing the competitors to undercut
the renegade's prices. 

(Why is Microsoft willing to lose so much money to foreign pirates?
Apparently, the cost of transferring a share of foreign license fees to
persons influential enough to change enforcement policy is greater than the
benefit to be gained by collecting the fees.)

Pirates could collude with a PA. If pirate publishers spring up in
sufficient numbers to keep the occupants of a province well-stocked in
stolen intellectual goods, all under the protection of the local PA, then
an unstable, parasitic process would ensue. Pirates would gain and
legitimate publishers lose, with a switch to the other legal setup a
possible outcome.

Now lets go back to Friedman's example, and ask whether a PA innovating
intellectual property will prosper and hence gain immitators. If the net
profit from having local enforcement of intellectual property is positive,
there should be some allocation of PA prices to customers, PA prices to
publisher, and publisher's retained profits that benefits everyone. While
the publishers do not have perfect knowledge of the relative profit
streams, and may encounter collective action problems, it seems plausible
that if large profits were to be had by enforcing intellectual property,
intellectual property would result.

Since both the gains and losses involved fall primarily on the same group,
anarchocapitalism will plausibly (but not certainly) produce legal rules
with or without intellectual property, depending on which is on net
desirable. The larger the gain offered by one over the other, the more
likely the correct decision will be taken.

An arms race between pirates and enforcers influences intellectual property
law. Before the photocopier came into widespread use, enforcing
intellectual property for printed matter was fairly low cost. Only those
possessing a printing press were a serious threat, and they were easy to
keep in line. Similarly, the advent of audio cassettes, video cassettes,
DAT, etc. make enforcement of intellectual property relatively costly these
days. But that is not to say that pirate - catching technology won't ever
catch up.

Dave Burns


